  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how PFAS-containing materials are managed and where problematic byproducts can emerge. At the top, it shows a general pathway: PFAS wastes may first go through non-destructive concentration methods, which produce concentrated PFAS residuals, and these residuals then enter destructive treatment. Destruction does not simply make the problem disappear; it can generate air emissions, liquid residuals, and solid residuals.\n\nThe lower diagrams compare three treatment system types. A thermal system with stacks uses a rotary kiln, afterburner, spray dryer, cyclone, filter, and acid scrubber. Along this flow, multiple outputs are identified, including bottom ash, powdered salt, fly ash, scrubbing liquid, and flue gas. Red labels marked “PIDs” appear on all of these streams, indicating products of incomplete destruction.\n\nThe other two panels show non-thermal destructive setups: a closed-vessel system and an open-vessel system. In both cases, PFAS-containing feed enters a treatment vessel, but the visible outputs again separate into gas, liquids, and solids, each also marked with PIDs. Overall, the figure emphasizes that across both thermal and non-thermal destruction technologies, PFAS treatment can shift contamination into several residual streams rather than fully eliminating it.",
